# The product of two balls with 2-limb centers: the center by mul_2_2, the radius in the second design.
# The radius is evaluated by operations rounded to nearest on nonnegative values and inflated
# once (milestone1.md, "The radius, second design"): E turns the error bound of the center
# kernel mul_2_2 into a multiple of |cz0|, G covers the roundings of the radius, H what underflow
# loses and the absolute term of the bound. Each is an exact expression rounded up.
kernel:        ball_mul_2
input:         cx = cx0 cx1
input:         stored rx
input:         cy = cy0 cy1
input:         stored ry
output:        cz = cz0 cz1
output:        rz
value:         cx*cy
requires:      normalized cx
requires:      nonneg rx
requires:      normalized cy
requires:      nonneg ry
ensures:       normalized cz
ensures:       encloses cz rz x*y x=cx,rx y=cy,ry
min_precision: 4
theorem:       proofs/theories/Ball2.v ball_mul_2_correct
source:        midpoint-radius product (Milestone 1 listing, second radius design)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the radius never reaches the center; the special states of the ball layer (Milestone 1 statements, Ball kernels) test the center and the radii

(cz0, cz1)  = mul_2_2(cx0, cx1, cy0, cy1)
ax          = abs(cx0)
ay          = abs(cy0)
az          = abs(cz0)
p           = prod(rx, ry)
q           = fma(ay, rx, p)
w           = fma(ax, ry, q)
t           = fma(az, const up(unit(mul_2_2)/(1-unit(mul_2_2))), w)
rz          = fma(t, const up((1+u)^6), const up((1+u)*(2*(1+u)^5*eta+tiny(mul_2_2)/(1-unit(mul_2_2))+eta/2)))
